                    <h1 class="content-title">Threat Intelligence & <span class="gradient-text">MITRE ATT&CK</span></h1>
                    <p class="content-subtitle">
                        Threat Intelligence provides context to security incidents by aggregating IoC data from 
                        external feeds, enriching observables, and mapping threats to the MITRE ATT&CK framework.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <section class="content-section" id="tisc">
                    <h2>Threat Intelligence Security Center (TISC)</h2>
                    <p>
                        <strong>TISC</strong> is the primary module for managing threat intelligence within SecOps. 
                        It centralizes all threat data ingestion, observable management, and enrichment workflows.
                    </p>
                    <h3>TISC Capabilities</h3>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Centralized IoC repository:</strong> All observables (IPs, domains, hashes, URLs) in one place</li>
                        <li><strong>Multi-source aggregation:</strong> Ingest from commercial feeds, open-source feeds, ISACs, and custom sources</li>
                        <li><strong>Automated enrichment:</strong> Flow Designer workflows that query VirusTotal, WHOIS, passive DNS, and more</li>
                        <li><strong>Threat scoring:</strong> Calculate threat scores based on multiple intelligence sources</li>
                        <li><strong>Incident correlation:</strong> Automatically link observables to security incidents</li>
                        <li><strong>Detection rules:</strong> Auto-extract MITRE techniques from incoming alert data</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>

                <section class="content-section" id="observables">
                    <h2>Observables & IoCs</h2>
                    <p>
                        <strong>Observables</strong> are the atomic units of threat intelligence — specific, 
                        identifiable indicators that may indicate malicious activity. Stored in 
                        <code>sn_ti_observable</code>.
                    </p>
                    <div class="data-table-wrapper">
                        <table class="data-table">
                            <thead><tr><th>Observable Type</th><th>Example</th><th>Common Source</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><strong>IP Address</strong></td><td>192.168.1.100, 203.0.113.50</td><td>SIEM alerts, firewall logs, threat feeds</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><strong>Domain</strong></td><td>malicious-domain.evil.com</td><td>DNS logs, phishing emails, threat feeds</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><strong>URL</strong></td><td>https://evil.com/payload.exe</td><td>Email analysis, web proxy logs</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><strong>File Hash (MD5/SHA256)</strong></td><td>a1b2c3d4e5f6...</td><td>EDR detections, malware analysis</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><strong>Email Address</strong></td><td>attacker@evil.com</td><td>Phishing incidents, email gateway</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><strong>CVE ID</strong></td><td>CVE-2024-1234</td><td>Vulnerability intelligence feeds</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>

                    <h3>Observable States</h3>
                    <div class="diagram-block">
                        <div class="diagram-title">Observable Processing Pipeline</div>
                        <div class="flow-container">
                            <div class="flow-step">
                                <span class="flow-step-label">New</span>
                                <span class="flow-step-desc">Observable created from alert/feed</span>
                            </div>
                            <span class="flow-arrow">→</span>
                            <div class="flow-step">
                                <span class="flow-step-label">Processed</span>
                                <span class="flow-step-desc">Validated and ready for enrichment</span>
                            </div>
                            <span class="flow-arrow">→</span>
                            <div class="flow-step">
                                <span class="flow-step-label">Enriched</span>
                                <span class="flow-step-desc">Threat intel lookups complete</span>
                            </div>
                            <span class="flow-arrow">→</span>
                            <div class="flow-step">
                                <span class="flow-step-label">Actioned</span>
                                <span class="flow-step-desc">Blocking/containment applied</span>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="content-section" id="enrichment">
                    <h2>Observable Enrichment</h2>
                    <p>
                        Enrichment is the process of querying external services to add context to an observable. 
                        This is typically automated via <strong>Flow Designer</strong>.
                    </p>
                    <h3>Common Enrichment Sources</h3>
                    <div class="accordion">
                        <div class="accordion-item open">
                            <button class="accordion-trigger"><span class="accordion-trigger-text">VirusTotal</span><span class="accordion-icon">▾</span></button>
                            <div class="accordion-body"><div class="accordion-content">
                                <p>Query file hashes, URLs, IPs, and domains against VirusTotal's multi-engine analysis. Returns detection ratios, malware family names, and community scores.</p>
                            </div></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="accordion-item">
                            <button class="accordion-trigger"><span class="accordion-trigger-text">WHOIS / Passive DNS</span><span class="accordion-icon">▾</span></button>
                            <div class="accordion-body"><div class="accordion-content">
                                <p>Look up domain registration details (registrar, creation date, registrant info) and historical DNS resolution data. Useful for identifying newly registered domains often used in phishing campaigns.</p>
                            </div></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="accordion-item">
                            <button class="accordion-trigger"><span class="accordion-trigger-text">CrowdStrike Falcon</span><span class="accordion-icon">▾</span></button>
                            <div class="accordion-body"><div class="accordion-content">
                                <p>Pull endpoint detection data, threat actor attribution, and adversary group intelligence. Can also be used to push containment actions (isolate host, kill process) back to the endpoint.</p>
                            </div></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="accordion-item">
                            <button class="accordion-trigger"><span class="accordion-trigger-text">MISP (Open Source)</span><span class="accordion-icon">▾</span></button>
                            <div class="accordion-body"><div class="accordion-content">
                                <p>Query the Malware Information Sharing Platform for community-shared IoCs, threat events, and correlation data. Popular for ISACs and government threat sharing programs.</p>
                            </div></div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="info-card info-card--tip">
                        <div class="info-card-title">Cost Optimization</div>
                        <p>
                            Filter out internal observables (private IPs, local MAC addresses, internal domains) 
                            <strong>before</strong> sending to enrichment APIs. External lookups have API rate 
                            limits and costs — querying your own 10.x.x.x addresses against VirusTotal wastes 
                            API calls.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="content-section" id="mitre">
                    <h2>MITRE ATT&CK Integration</h2>
                    <p>
                        ServiceNow includes native integration with the <strong>MITRE ATT&CK</strong> framework, 
                        allowing you to classify threats using standardized Tactics, Techniques, and Procedures (TTPs).
                    </p>
                    <h3>How It Works</h3>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Technique database:</strong> The <code>sn_ti_stix2_attack_pattern</code> table contains all MITRE ATT&CK techniques</li>
                        <li><strong>Incident mapping:</strong> Analysts can associate techniques with security incidents to classify the attack</li>
                        <li><strong>Auto-extraction:</strong> Detection rules can automatically extract MITRE technique IDs from SIEM alert data</li>
                        <li><strong>Coverage analysis:</strong> Visualize which ATT&CK techniques your security controls cover (and where gaps exist)</li>
                        <li><strong>Script Include:</strong> <code>sn_ti.MITREUtil</code> provides programmatic access to add techniques to incidents</li>
                    </ul>

                    <h3>MITRE ATT&CK Tactics</h3>
                    <div class="data-table-wrapper">
                        <table class="data-table">
                            <thead><tr><th>Tactic</th><th>Description</th><th>Example Techniques</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><strong>Initial Access</strong></td><td>How the attacker got in</td><td>Phishing, Exploit Public App, Valid Accounts</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><strong>Execution</strong></td><td>Running malicious code</td><td>PowerShell, Command Line, Scripting</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><strong>Persistence</strong></td><td>Maintaining foothold</td><td>Registry Run Keys, Scheduled Tasks, Boot Kits</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><strong>Privilege Escalation</strong></td><td>Gaining higher access</td><td>Token Manipulation, Exploiting Vulns, Sudo</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><strong>Defense Evasion</strong></td><td>Avoiding detection</td><td>Obfuscation, Rootkits, Timestomping</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><strong>Credential Access</strong></td><td>Stealing credentials</td><td>Keylogging, Brute Force, Credential Dumping</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><strong>Lateral Movement</strong></td><td>Moving through network</td><td>RDP, SMB, Pass-the-Hash</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><strong>Exfiltration</strong></td><td>Stealing data</td><td>C2 Channel, Cloud Storage, USB</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><strong>Impact</strong></td><td>Disruption or destruction</td><td>Ransomware, Data Destruction, DDoS</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="content-section" id="feeds">
                    <h2>Threat Feeds</h2>
                    <p>
                        ServiceNow supports ingesting threat data from multiple sources:
                    </p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Commercial feeds:</strong> Recorded Future, Anomali, ThreatConnect, Mandiant</li>
                        <li><strong>Open-source feeds:</strong> AlienVault OTX, Abuse.ch, MISP communities</li>
                        <li><strong>Government/ISAC feeds:</strong> US-CERT, FS-ISAC, MS-ISAC</li>
                        <li><strong>Internal feeds:</strong> Custom IoC lists from your own threat hunting team</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>

                <section class="content-section" id="stix-taxii">
                    <h2>STIX & TAXII Standards</h2>
                    <p>
                        ServiceNow supports the industry-standard <strong>STIX (Structured Threat Information 
                        Expression)</strong> and <strong>TAXII (Trusted Automated Exchange of Intelligence 
                        Information)</strong> protocols for threat intelligence sharing.
                    </p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>STIX 2.x:</strong> Data format standard for expressing threat intelligence objects (indicators, attack patterns, malware, threat actors)</li>
                        <li><strong>TAXII Client:</strong> ServiceNow includes a built-in TAXII client for automated polling of STIX data from TAXII servers</li>
                        <li><strong>Custom parsers:</strong> Support for TEXT, CSV, and JSON format feeds with configurable mapping rules</li>
                    </ul>
                    <div class="info-card info-card--important">
                        <div class="info-card-title">STIX Tables</div>
                        <p>
                            STIX objects are stored in the <code>sn_ti_stix2_*</code> table family. This includes 
                            <code>sn_ti_stix2_indicator</code>, <code>sn_ti_stix2_attack_pattern</code>, 
                            <code>sn_ti_stix2_malware</code>, and <code>sn_ti_stix2_threat_actor</code>. 
                            These tables follow the STIX 2.x specification structure.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </section>

